 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Selective Clamp
-A mitigation technique that clamps only the scale and opacity fields to prevent catastrophic upsets, relying on the fact that these fields carry almost all of the catastrophic mass [S1, unofficial]. It is cheaper than the full support guard, with a cost of 1× memory and <0.1 ms/frame, and is nearly as effective [S1, unofficial].
+A mitigation technique that clamps only the scale and opacity fields to prevent catastrophic upsets, relying on the fact that these fields carry almost all of the catastrophic mass [S1, unofficial]. It is cheaper than the full support guard, with a cost of 1× memory and <0.1 ms/frame, and nearly as effective in reducing catastrophe rates [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The guard cost grows close to linearly with the primitive count, as it is a bounded pass over the parameters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory overhead for the guard itself, as it operates on existing parameters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a system with 66 million primitives, the guard cost is 9.66 ms per frame, which is 18.3% of the render time [S1, unofficial]. The guard sustains an effective parameter-read bandwidth of 763 GB/s [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** In a 2-GPU system, a single scale-sign upset contaminates both GPUs without the guard, but the node-local guard confines the corruption to 1 node [S1, unofficial]. On a 4-GPU system, the guard similarly confines the upset to 1 node [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates catastrophic upsets with no memory overhead; complements ECC by catching errors in registers/caches; low per-frame cost [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not protect against errors within the support box; effectiveness weakens with very large primitives [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** In distributed multi-GPU rendering environments where bit-flips can cause catastrophic spatial extent explosions and cross-node contamination [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory overhead is strictly zero and no periodic scrubbing is acceptable, or when the model contains intentionally large primitives that exceed the support box bounds [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The guard cost grows close to linearly with the primitive count, as it performs a bounded pass over the parameters [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** 1× memory overhead for storing the support box bounds (negligible compared to primitive data) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a system with 66 million primitives, the guard cost was 9.66 ms per frame (18.3% of render time) when running every frame. When deployed as a periodic scrub, the per-frame cost amortizes toward zero [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Effectiveness:** Reduces the mean scale-sign footprint from 0.0880% (unguarded) to 0.0035% (guarded) and eliminates catastrophic upsets (0.000% catastrophe rate) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** No cross-node traffic or synchronization overhead; complements ECC by catching semantic errors; low per-frame cost if used periodically [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Does not protect against bit-flips that remain within the support box; effectiveness weakens if model optimization creates very large primitives [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** In distributed or multi-GPU rendering systems where silent data corruption (e.g., from radiation or hardware faults) can cause catastrophic visual artifacts and load imbalance. Particularly useful when ECC is insufficient or too costly [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The node-local support guard was introduced in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering reliability under single-event upsets [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the problem of silent data corruption in high-performance computing environments, particularly in space or high-radiation settings where bit-flips are more common [S1, unofficial]. The method is complementary to ECC and full duplication, offering a middle ground in terms of cost and effectiveness [S1, unofficial].
+The Node-Local Support Guard was introduced in research on the reliability of 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering under single-event upsets (SEUs) [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the problem of "contamination," where a bit-flip in one primitive's parameters causes it to explode in size, affecting multiple nodes in a distributed renderer and causing load imbalance [S1, unofficial]. The method was validated on real hardware, including 2-GPU and 4-GPU systems, demonstrating its ability to confine corruption to a single node [S1, unofficial].
 
